WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.760 --> 00:00:04.700
When Speed was released in 1994, an action
star was born.

00:00:04.940 --> 00:00:08.420
Since then, Keanu Reeves has appeared in a
number of action movies that have put his

00:00:08.430 --> 00:00:09.600
abilities to good use.

00:00:09.780 --> 00:00:13.160
Some of them are stone-cold classics, while
others… aren’t.

00:00:13.580 --> 00:00:18.080
Here’s every one of Reeves’ action movies,
ranked worst to best.

00:00:18.820 --> 00:00:23.980
Based on the William Gibson cyberpunk novel
of the same name, 1995's Johnny Mnemonic follows

00:00:23.980 --> 00:00:28.060
the story of a man with a cybernetic brain
implant that helps him store information.

00:00:28.160 --> 00:00:33.300
As is the standard for Gibson novels, humanity's
reliance on technology and the dystopian corporate

00:00:33.300 --> 00:00:37.100
environment of this future keep people in
a kind of endless servitude.

00:00:37.160 --> 00:00:41.720
Reeves stars in the title role, with supporting
performances from Dolph Lundgren and Ice-T.

00:00:41.980 --> 00:00:46.479
Sadly, Johnny Mnemonic's premise just isn't
exciting to watch, despite the action being

00:00:46.480 --> 00:00:47.200
pretty heavy.

00:00:47.420 --> 00:00:51.300
As Johnny tries to recover his own childhood
memories that have been replaced by years

00:00:51.309 --> 00:00:55.929
of data smuggling, the main conflict loses
tangibility, and no amount of action is enough

00:00:55.929 --> 00:00:57.480
to make us care about his plight.

00:00:57.600 --> 00:01:01.820
Johnny Mnemonic is basically a guy running
around a sci-fi future trying to remember

00:01:01.820 --> 00:01:06.300
something he forgot, which doesn't exactly
make for a great cinematic experience.

00:01:07.260 --> 00:01:11.300
47 Ronin was one of the biggest box office
bombs of 2013.

00:01:11.560 --> 00:01:16.560
The overly-ambitious project reportedly fell
victim to a constantly-shifting focus in production

00:01:16.560 --> 00:01:20.000
and a lack of experience on the part of first-time
director Carl Rinsch.

00:01:20.200 --> 00:01:24.220
There was also a debate around the project
about the balance between Eastern themes and

00:01:24.220 --> 00:01:26.700
a perceived need for "more Western touches."

00:01:26.780 --> 00:01:30.920
One of those Western touches was the addition
of Reeves to a mostly Japanese cast of the

00:01:30.920 --> 00:01:35.960
film, which retells an old legend about 47
rogue samurai avenging the death of their

00:01:35.960 --> 00:01:36.460
master.

00:01:36.620 --> 00:01:41.140
What really brought 47 Ronin down was that,
despite its well-choreographed fight scenes

00:01:41.140 --> 00:01:46.200
and slick use of 3D and CGI special effects,
the movie was surprisingly dull, with little

00:01:46.200 --> 00:01:49.680
to no development from Reeves or any of the
other one-dimensional characters.

00:01:49.940 --> 00:01:54.280
But don't worry — Reeves did go on to make
another martial arts movie in 2013 that turned

00:01:54.280 --> 00:01:56.770
out much, much better than 47 Ronin.

00:01:56.770 --> 00:01:57.540
We'll get there.

00:01:58.120 --> 00:02:03.660
1996's Chain Reaction, starring Keanu Reeves
and Morgan Freeman, suffers simply from being

00:02:03.660 --> 00:02:04.400
confusing.

00:02:04.660 --> 00:02:09.300
Reeves stars as a machinist who discovers
some new convoluted thing about hydrogen energy

00:02:09.500 --> 00:02:13.680
— before his lab explodes and he and his
colleague are framed for the bombing.

00:02:13.920 --> 00:02:18.200
Freeman plays the wealthy doctor funding their
project, to whom they turn for help, though

00:02:18.200 --> 00:02:21.720
of course he turns out — perhaps inevitably
— to be the bad guy.

00:02:21.880 --> 00:02:26.760
But much like Johnny Mnemonic, Chain Reaction
has no tether for the audience to grasp onto

00:02:26.760 --> 00:02:27.560
for stabilization.

00:02:27.820 --> 00:02:31.200
Reeves' character is framed for something
that isn't clear, while Freeman's character

00:02:31.200 --> 00:02:33.520
is after something that never really becomes
clear, either.

00:02:33.640 --> 00:02:37.980
It's a mess of a movie whose only saving graces
are a few good action sequences.

00:02:38.140 --> 00:02:42.379
There were a lot of these formulaic action
movies in the '90s — ones that all had something

00:02:42.379 --> 00:02:44.730
to do with science or government or spies.

00:02:44.730 --> 00:02:47.900
Unfortunately, Chain Reaction wasn't one of
the good ones.

00:02:48.520 --> 00:02:53.439
The final film in the Wachowskis' Matrix trilogy
has the benefit of bringing the epic story

00:02:53.439 --> 00:02:57.769
to a close, granting it the opportunity to
provide a rousing climax and closure to its

00:02:57.769 --> 00:02:58.920
philosophical journey.

00:02:59.100 --> 00:03:03.920
Sadly, that didn't save it from receiving
the worst reviews of the series, largely because

00:03:03.920 --> 00:03:07.120
it sucks pretty much all the fun out of the
entire franchise.

00:03:07.220 --> 00:03:11.660
Not only does Revolutions end on a downer
of a conclusion — with Trinity killed in

00:03:11.670 --> 00:03:15.930
action and Neo sacrificing himself for peace
between the humans and the machines — but

00:03:15.930 --> 00:03:18.920
the final showdown turned out pretty underwhelming
in itself.

00:03:19.220 --> 00:03:25.249
The program known as Agent Smith, played brilliantly
by Hugo Weaving, wants to destroy the Matrix,

00:03:25.249 --> 00:03:29.909
the machine world, and the human world, for
reasons that never really become totally clear

00:03:29.909 --> 00:03:33.540
— other than that he's a broken outcast
and wants revenge.

00:03:33.829 --> 00:03:38.939
A rogue virus, Agent Smith was apparently
supposed to be deactivated, but has instead

00:03:38.940 --> 00:03:41.880
decided to make it his goal to kill literally
everybody.

00:03:42.080 --> 00:03:46.460
“It was your life that taught me the purpose
of all life.

00:03:47.360 --> 00:03:49.620
The purpose of life is to end.”

00:03:51.640 --> 00:03:55.620
In amongst all this, the characters we know
and care about kind of get lost in a jumble

00:03:55.620 --> 00:04:00.060
of side characters, overwhelming robot battle
sequences, and convoluted plotlines.

00:04:00.309 --> 00:04:04.900
The Matrix movies can always be proud of their
seriously cool action scenes and intriguing

00:04:04.900 --> 00:04:07.700
ideas, but this one was a bit of a bummer.

00:04:12.720 --> 00:04:15.980
Constantine is one of those movies that should
have been really cool, but just didn't live

00:04:15.980 --> 00:04:17.400
up to its potential.

00:04:17.820 --> 00:04:22.780
Based on DC Comics' Hellblazer books, Constantine
tells the story of a mortal human who can

00:04:22.780 --> 00:04:27.020
see the celestial angels and demons who infiltrate
and manipulate humanity.

00:04:27.200 --> 00:04:32.030
Rachel Weisz co-stars as a police detective
whose sister's death reveals a plot that upsets

00:04:32.030 --> 00:04:34.040
the balance between the living and the dead.

00:04:34.360 --> 00:04:38.220
The movie’s got a great cast, including
an androgynous Archangel Gabriel played by

00:04:38.220 --> 00:04:41.380
Tilda Swinton, and some of the fight scenes
are really fun, too.

00:04:41.600 --> 00:04:46.060
But ultimately, the plot isn't one that translates
particularly well onto the screen.

00:04:46.140 --> 00:04:50.140
As a result, the movie is simultaneously convoluted
and predictable.

00:04:50.380 --> 00:04:55.480
Roger Ebert gave the film one-and-a-half stars,
while his TV co-host Richard Roeper wrote:

00:04:55.480 --> 00:04:58.660
"It's just so awful that the crew must have
been snickering."

00:04:59.400 --> 00:05:04.840
While 1999's The Matrix was hailed as an exciting
new entry into the sci-fi genre with groundbreaking

00:05:04.840 --> 00:05:09.590
filmmaking techniques, its two sequels weren't
nearly as well-received as that first hit.

00:05:09.590 --> 00:05:14.150
The two Matrix sequels weren't great mainly
because, much like too many others films these

00:05:14.150 --> 00:05:18.530
days, they felt like they should have been
a single movie — but the plot had expanded

00:05:18.530 --> 00:05:22.220
and bloated to the point where the powers
that be decided two would be more exciting

00:05:22.220 --> 00:05:23.220
for audiences.

00:05:23.220 --> 00:05:28.280
The second movie in the trilogy, 2003's The
Matrix Reloaded, was praised for its action

00:05:28.280 --> 00:05:32.650
and style but panned for its portrayal of
the humans who live in Zion, humanity's haven

00:05:32.650 --> 00:05:33.840
outside of the Matrix.

00:05:34.060 --> 00:05:38.460
One infamous scene in particular has been
subject to some pretty intense mockery since

00:05:38.460 --> 00:05:39.440
the movie’s release.

00:05:45.280 --> 00:05:50.100
Between that and Reloaded's over-reliance
on philosophical conversations at the expense

00:05:50.110 --> 00:05:54.440
of the action, it simply ends up failing to
live up to the first Matrix movie — despite

00:05:54.440 --> 00:05:56.220
Reeves' excellent fighting skills.

00:05:56.820 --> 00:06:00.760
Many actors end up dipping their toes into
the world of directing, but not all of them

00:06:00.760 --> 00:06:01.660
are any good at it.

00:06:01.960 --> 00:06:05.940
Thankfully, Reeves' first directorial outing
was actually pretty decent.

00:06:05.940 --> 00:06:10.520
Man of Tai Chi, a Chinese-American martial
arts film, is a love letter to the film’s

00:06:10.530 --> 00:06:15.430
star and Reeves’ stuntman friend, Tiger
Chen, and is inspired by his life and experiences.

00:06:15.430 --> 00:06:20.300
Man of Tai Chi came out the same year as 47
Ronin, but managed to avoid the same mistakes

00:06:20.300 --> 00:06:21.620
that brought that movie down.

00:06:21.940 --> 00:06:26.500
For one thing, the movie actually embraced
its Chinese roots, cast, and environment,

00:06:26.500 --> 00:06:30.120
rather than a shoehorning a white, Western
viewpoint into an ancient story.

00:06:30.370 --> 00:06:34.600
The critical consensus was that Man of Tai
Chi didn't do anything groundbreaking, per

00:06:34.610 --> 00:06:38.240
se, but that the story hearkened back to a
style of martial arts movie that hadn't been

00:06:38.240 --> 00:06:43.200
seen in quite some time, which Reeves lovingly
embraced and paid tribute to in his directorial

00:06:43.200 --> 00:06:43.800
debut.

00:06:44.000 --> 00:06:45.140
The New York Times wrote:

00:06:45.520 --> 00:06:50.340
"The movie finds a Januslike Mr. Reeves looking
in two directions at once: toward the old-school

00:06:50.350 --> 00:06:54.710
kung-fu movies of once upon a matinee time
and the modern China emblemized by the high

00:06:54.710 --> 00:06:57.780
rises that slice through smog-swathed Beijing."

00:06:58.080 --> 00:07:02.360
Reeves' respect for China, and the style of
filmmaking that served as a personal tribute

00:07:02.360 --> 00:07:04.700
to his friend, is on very clear display, here.

00:07:05.460 --> 00:07:10.480
If ever there was a spiritual successor to
Ted Logan of Bill and Ted's Excellent Adventure,

00:07:10.480 --> 00:07:13.520
it's surfer FBI agent Johnny Utah of Point
Break.

00:07:13.680 --> 00:07:17.580
The film, directed by future Oscar winner
Kathryn Bigelow, tells the story of a gang

00:07:17.580 --> 00:07:22.860
of crooks known as the "Ex-Presidents" — because
they wear rubber masks of Nixon, Reagan, Johnson,

00:07:22.860 --> 00:07:24.520
and Carter while they rob banks.

00:07:24.680 --> 00:07:29.670
They also happen to be expert surfers, so
rookie undercover agent Utah infiltrates their

00:07:29.670 --> 00:07:32.540
ranks, eventually embracing his destiny as
a wave rider.

00:07:32.800 --> 00:07:36.340
In addition to having some thrilling action
and killer surfing scenes, Point Break is

00:07:36.340 --> 00:07:40.330
also a lovely reminder of the acting talent
of Patrick Swayze, who passed away

00:07:40.330 --> 00:07:42.580
in 2009 from pancreatic cancer.

00:07:43.160 --> 00:07:45.140
"He was a beautiful person, an artist.

00:07:45.320 --> 00:07:48.840
Patrick, he just wanted to experience life…
he lived life to the fullest."

00:07:49.760 --> 00:07:53.480
Granted, both John Wick movies are great,
and it’s pretty hard to rank one above the

00:07:53.480 --> 00:07:53.980
other.

00:07:54.240 --> 00:07:58.040
John Wick: Chapter 2 comes in just behind
the first here because the first acted so

00:07:58.040 --> 00:08:00.680
well as a kind of a surprise comeback film
for Reeves.

00:08:00.880 --> 00:08:04.920
John Wick: Chapter 2 begins just a few days
after the events of John Wick, and finds the

00:08:04.920 --> 00:08:09.660
former assassin continuing his rampage of
revenge — only this time with a new dog.

00:08:09.930 --> 00:08:14.460
The film features just as much sleek, well-directed
violence as the first film, but John Wick:

00:08:14.460 --> 00:08:18.860
Chapter 2 also takes a trip over to Rome for
some international espionage among the ancient

00:08:18.860 --> 00:08:23.470
ruins of the eternal city — proving that
Wick is a truly worldly international assassin.

00:08:24.180 --> 00:08:27.580
“Whoever comes, whoever it is…

00:08:28.760 --> 00:08:29.720
I’ll kill them.

00:08:31.140 --> 00:08:32.180
I’ll kill them all.”

00:08:33.420 --> 00:08:38.100
Despite having starred in numerous action
movies, John Wick might be Keanu Reeves' grand

00:08:38.100 --> 00:08:39.060
opus of a role.

00:08:39.510 --> 00:08:44.440
This 2014 neo-noir action film tells the story
of a former assassin, drawn back into a life

00:08:44.440 --> 00:08:48.880
of killing after car thieves kill his puppy
— who was a memento left behind by his late

00:08:48.880 --> 00:08:49.480
wife.

00:08:49.600 --> 00:08:54.300
The role is absolutely perfect for Reeves,
whose sleek looks, naturally stoic nature,

00:08:54.300 --> 00:08:57.660
and incredible action skills are all put to
great use in the movie.

00:08:57.860 --> 00:09:01.880
Everyone was kind of surprised by how good
John Wick was, and how perfect Reeves was

00:09:01.880 --> 00:09:02.500
for the part.

00:09:02.700 --> 00:09:06.180
It was labelled as a career comeback for Reeves,
whose star had kind of dwindled.

00:09:06.520 --> 00:09:08.780
In 2017, Birth.Movies.Death wrote:

00:09:08.900 --> 00:09:13.220
"It wasn't too long ago that critics were
bemoaning the presumed end of Reeves' career

00:09:13.220 --> 00:09:14.180
as a leading man."

00:09:14.650 --> 00:09:18.440
But the John Wick franchise has helped bring
Reeves back into the spotlight — and now

00:09:18.440 --> 00:09:20.140
he's calling the shots.

00:09:20.260 --> 00:09:21.480
“You, uh, working again?”

00:09:21.900 --> 00:09:23.600
“Nah, just sorting stuff out.”

00:09:24.200 --> 00:09:28.760
Never before and never since has a city bus
had such a grand movie moment.

00:09:28.840 --> 00:09:34.100
Speed is the film that put Reeves on the action
radar, and is still a great watch 25 years

00:09:34.100 --> 00:09:34.600
later.

00:09:34.780 --> 00:09:39.660
Reeves stars as Los Angeles SWAT officer Jack,
who boards an LA bus that's rigged with a

00:09:39.660 --> 00:09:43.480
bomb that will explode if the vehicle's speed
drops below 50 miles per hour.

00:09:43.770 --> 00:09:47.560
Sandra Bullock co-stars as a passenger named
Annie who takes over driving duties while

00:09:47.560 --> 00:09:50.420
Jack tries to defuse the bomb and save the
other passengers.

00:09:50.660 --> 00:09:55.100
Speed's premise is a tad ridiculous, and there
are plenty of seriously unbelievable moments

00:09:55.110 --> 00:09:59.090
throughout the movie, but its absurdity is
overshadowed by the fact that it's so just

00:09:59.090 --> 00:09:59.880
darned fun.

00:10:00.100 --> 00:10:04.540
Reeves and Bullock have great chemistry, and
his action prowess here is accompanied by

00:10:04.540 --> 00:10:06.440
a real sense of charm and acting charisma.

00:10:07.140 --> 00:10:11.140
This is the movie for which Reeves will be
remembered — for the rest of his life and

00:10:11.140 --> 00:10:11.640
beyond.

00:10:11.780 --> 00:10:16.260
It's also the franchise that put sci-fi masters
the Wachowski Sisters on the movie map.

00:10:16.520 --> 00:10:21.390
By utilizing new filmmaking technology and
tapping into the exploding Internet zeitgeist,

00:10:21.390 --> 00:10:25.160
The Matrix was able to become an action movie
for the budding new millennium.

00:10:25.600 --> 00:10:30.500
Reeves stars as Thomas Anderson, a computer
programmer who lives a double life online

00:10:30.500 --> 00:10:31.680
as the hacker "Neo."

00:10:31.860 --> 00:10:35.920
After he's contacted by Morpheus and Trinity,
he finally learns the truth about the world.

00:10:36.040 --> 00:10:40.800
Artificial intelligence has taken over the
planet and robot overlords use humans for

00:10:40.810 --> 00:10:45.270
their bio energy, keeping them in a catatonic
state while their minds believe they are living

00:10:45.270 --> 00:10:46.200
in the regular world.

00:10:46.580 --> 00:10:51.610
This false consciousness is called "The Matrix,"
and Neo joins the quest to free humanity from

00:10:51.610 --> 00:10:52.280
its grasp.

00:10:52.360 --> 00:10:57.200
The movie used innovative camera techniques
to amp up the many awesome fight scenes, including

00:10:57.210 --> 00:11:01.880
360-degree camera movement and extreme slow
motion to make even more of a mind bender

00:11:01.880 --> 00:11:03.630
out of an already interesting premise.

00:11:03.780 --> 00:11:08.020
By utilizing Reeves' natural stoic nature
and his sleek movements, The Matrix became

00:11:08.020 --> 00:11:09.900
his best ever action movie.

